{"name":"Manchester Brown","hex":"#504440","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"manchester","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A type of cotton fabric, or a piece of clothing made from such fabric. .mw-parser-output .defdate{font-size:smaller}","Household linen, especially as a category of goods in a store."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/manchester"},{"term":"brown","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A colour like that of chocolate or coffee.","One of the colour balls used in snooker, with a value of 4 points."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Having a brown color/colour.","Gloomy."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/brown"}],"entries":[{"title":"Bismarck brown Y","blocks":[{"text":"Bismarck brown Y also called C.I. 21000 and C.I. Basic Brown 1, is a diazo dye with the idealized formula [(H2N)2C6H3N2]2C6H4. The dye is a mixture of closely related compounds. It was one of the earliest azo dyes, being described in 1863 by German chemist Carl Alexander von Martius. Martius named the dye after German chancellor Otto von Bismarck. It is used in histology for staining tissues."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bismarck_brown_Y","wikidata":"Q412880"},{"title":"Manchester","blocks":[{"text":"Manchester is a city and metropolitan borough in North West England, at the centre of the eponymous county of Greater Manchester. It had a population of over 589,000 in 2024. It borders the Cheshire Plain to the south, the Pennines to the north and east, and the city of Salford to the west. The two cities and the surrounding towns form one of the United Kingdom's most populous conurbations, the Greater Manchester Built-up Area, which has a population of 2.87 million."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8c/Tower_Blocks_over_Knott_Mill%2C_geograph_6866152_by_David_Dixon.jpg/330px-Tower_Blocks_over_Knott_Mill%2C_geograph_6866152_by_David_Dixon.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.o